{
  "editions": [
    {
      "edition": "standard",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": false,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "speaker_attribution": true,
        "source_choices_and_notes": false,
        "terminology_policy": true
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "ὅσους, φησί, λίθους εἶδες εἰς τὴν οἰκοδομὴν | τοῦ πύργου εἰσεληλυθότας, ἐπιδεδομένους διὰ τῶν χειρῶν αὐτῶν καὶ μείναντας εἰς τὴν οἰκοδομήν, | τούτων τῶν παρθένων τὴν δύναμιν ἐνδεδυμένοι εἰσί.",
          "english_quote": "Without it: “All the stones you saw in the building are clothed in the power of these virgins.”",
          "issue": "The stated shorter alternative is not simply the selected Greek minus its bar-marked supplement: εἰσεληλυθότας, meaning having entered, is itself inside the marked span. Removing the entire span leaves a compressed construction whose rendering needs explanation. The supplied project report repeats this English alternative but does not supply the underlying shorter witness text or explain its syntax. The note presents a resolved alternative without disclosing that limitation.",
          "suggested_revision": "Identify this explicitly as the shorter rendering reported by the supplied project comparison, rather than an independently established translation obtained merely by removing the marked words. Supply the shorter Greek or versional text and its syntactic analysis if a direct witness translation is intended. Retain the disclosed working supplement in the main text.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "διὰ τοῦτο βλέπεις τὸν πύργον μονόλιθον γεγονότα [μετὰ] τῆς πέτρας.",
          "english_quote": "“With” is bracketed in the working Greek, inserted by Hilgenfeld compared with the Latin traditions; Athos is reported to omit it, and Ethiopic differs.",
          "issue": "The brackets are directly supported by the selected source, but the supplied evidence does not provide the apparatus entry supporting the attribution to Hilgenfeld or the stated Latin, Athos, and Ethiopic readings for this clause. The bounded comparison report addresses the long supplement in paragraph 4, not these paragraph 5 particulars. These specific provenance claims therefore remain unsupported within the review materials.",
          "suggested_revision": "Supply an attributed apparatus transcription or bounded report for paragraph 5 documenting these particulars, or limit the note to the directly available fact that “with” is bracketed in the working Greek. This does not require removing “with” from the main translation.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "Αὗται, φησίν, ἅγια πνεύματά εἰσι· καὶ ἄλλως ἄνθρωπος οὐ δύναται εὑρεθῆναι εἰς τὴν βασιλείαν τοῦ Θεοῦ, ἐὰν μὴ αὗται αὐτὸν ἐνδύσωσι τὸ ἔνδυμα αὐτῶν",
          "english_quote": "Retain the narrated affection, sibling distinction, the Son’s role and the stated gate/name and clothing requirements.",
          "issue": "The theological metadata claims to retain narrated affection and a sibling distinction, neither of which occurs in this selected section. It consequently misdescribes the source-specific grounds for this edition, even though the main translation does not introduce those claims.",
          "suggested_revision": "Replace the carried-over metadata with a section-specific account of the plural holy spirits, the Son’s powers, the name and clothing requirements, tower unity, and the subsequent exchange of powers. Any revised metadata requires fresh review.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "edition": "simplified",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": false,
        "no_added_claims": false,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "speaker_attribution": true,
        "source_choices_and_notes": false,
        "terminology_policy": true
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "ἐπιδεδομένους διὰ τῶν χειρῶν αὐτῶν",
          "english_quote": "pass through their hands",
          "issue": "The Greek describes the stones as handed over or delivered through the virgins’ hands. “Pass through” preserves movement but drops the explicit handing-over action. This defect is independently visible against the selected working supplement, regardless of its witness status.",
          "suggested_revision": "Consider “be handed over through their hands” within the sentence, preserving the delivery action. Review the resulting complete sentence against the Greek before acceptance.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "λαβόντες οὖν τὰ πνεύματα ταῦτα ἐνεδυναμώθησαν",
          "english_quote": "These spirits strengthened them.",
          "issue": "The source explicitly says that they received these spirits and were then strengthened. The simplified sentence omits their reception of the spirits and makes the spirits the explicit grammatical agent of strengthening, whereas the Greek leaves that passive agent unstated. Earlier reception of the virgins’ power does not translate this separate reception clause.",
          "suggested_revision": "Consider “After receiving these spirits, they were strengthened.” Preserve both reception and strengthening without specifying an unstated agent.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ταύτας ἰδόντες ἐπεθύμησαν αὐτῶν",
          "english_quote": "The people desired them.",
          "issue": "The simplified edition omits the people's seeing the women as the circumstance leading to desire. The preceding statement that the narrator saw the women has a different subject and does not cover this clause.",
          "suggested_revision": "Consider “When the people saw these women, they desired them.” Review the replacement in its narrative context.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ἐὰν [οὖν] τὸ ὄνομα φορῇς",
          "english_quote": "“So” later in this paragraph is bracketed in the printed source.",
          "issue": "The copied note refers to an English “So” later in the paragraph, but the simplified paragraph contains no such word. It therefore inaccurately describes how this edition handles the bracketed connective.",
          "suggested_revision": "Explain that the Greek connective meaning “so” or “therefore” is bracketed and is not separately rendered in this simplified edition, or propose a source-compatible rendering that includes it and submit that rendering for fresh review.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ὅσους, φησί, λίθους εἶδες εἰς τὴν οἰκοδομὴν | τοῦ πύργου εἰσεληλυθότας, ἐπιδεδομένους διὰ τῶν χειρῶν αὐτῶν καὶ μείναντας εἰς τὴν οἰκοδομήν, | τούτων τῶν παρθένων τὴν δύναμιν ἐνδεδυμένοι εἰσί.",
          "english_quote": "Without it: “All the stones you saw in the building are clothed in the power of these virgins.”",
          "issue": "The shorter alternative is supplied by the project report, but the underlying shorter witness wording and syntactic explanation are not supplied. Because εἰσεληλυθότας is inside the editorial span, deleting that span leaves a compressed construction rather than an independently demonstrated English alternative. The note does not distinguish the reported rendering from a directly reviewed shorter witness translation.",
          "suggested_revision": "Attribute the shorter English explicitly to the supplied project comparison and disclose its evidential limit, or supply the shorter source wording and analysis. Do not replace the selected working supplement merely because this alternative exists.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "διὰ τοῦτο βλέπεις τὸν πύργον μονόλιθον γεγονότα [μετὰ] τῆς πέτρας.",
          "english_quote": "“With” is bracketed in the working Greek, inserted by Hilgenfeld compared with the Latin traditions; Athos is reported to omit it, and Ethiopic differs.",
          "issue": "The selected source confirms the brackets, but no supplied apparatus entry or bounded comparison establishes these particular editorial and witness attributions for paragraph 5. The paragraph 4 evidence cannot establish them.",
          "suggested_revision": "Provide an attributed paragraph 5 apparatus transcription or report, or restrict the note to the visible bracketed status of “with.” Retaining the disclosed working reading is appropriate pending that evidence.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "Αὗται, φησίν, ἅγια πνεύματά εἰσι· καὶ ἄλλως ἄνθρωπος οὐ δύναται εὑρεθῆναι εἰς τὴν βασιλείαν τοῦ Θεοῦ, ἐὰν μὴ αὗται αὐτὸν ἐνδύσωσι τὸ ἔνδυμα αὐτῶν",
          "english_quote": "Retain ancient repair materials, sibling relationship and affection, gate/name requirements, holy-spirits plural and clothing imagery; preserve all substantive alternative notes.",
          "issue": "The simplification metadata claims preservation of ancient repair materials, a sibling relationship, and affection, none of which occurs in this selected section. The theological metadata likewise claims narrated affection and a sibling distinction. These are inaccurate section-specific audit claims.",
          "suggested_revision": "Replace both carried-over metadata descriptions with decisions actually applicable to section xiii, including preservation of reception, delivery, clothing imagery, necessity, plural spirits, and the exchange of powers. Submit the revised descriptions for fresh review.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Both editions require revision. The standard main text covers the selected working source and preserves its difficult assertions, necessity, negations, and servant terminology; its defects concern metadata and evidential disclosure. The simplified edition additionally loses specific delivery, reception, and seeing clauses. The reported paragraph 4 supplement should remain distinguished from preserved wording, not automatically discarded. This review evaluates the supplied Greek and attributed reports only; it does not authenticate unseen manuscripts or certify exhaustive collation. Proposed corrections remain subject to fresh review."
}
